# Test: a VALUELESS directive followed by a stray token is refused —
# `directive trace single`.
#
# The twin of `directive_space_separated_value_error.gg` (`directive scheduler
# single`), and the one that shows why the trailing-token check has to exist in
# the PARSER rather than being folded into the semantic set check. Deleting the
# space-separated branch alone turns `directive scheduler single` into a
# valueless `scheduler`, which the semantic pass refuses anyway — but it turns
# `directive trace single` into a valueless `trace`, which is LEGAL. Without the
# trailing-token check this row would silently ACCEPT, dropping the word the
# author wrote (Core #10).
#
# Rust gg refuses it: `single` is not consumed by `parse_directive`, so the item
# loop tries to parse it and fails.
#
# ⚠ Not in the divergence matrix `todo/t0825` was filed with — this and
# `directive_name_not_identifier_error.gg` are the 14th and 15th divergent cells,
# found by varying the grammar axis rather than the name/value sets.
#
# Pinned on BOTH lanes: `directive_trailing_token_error` (Rust gg) and
# `sh_rejects_directive_trailing_token` (self-host driver).

directive trace single

void main():
    print("REACHED — the trailing directive token was NOT refused")
